```

Events are also published to NATS: `routine-resolver.sessions.{id}.events`.

The create envelope may carry `client`: up to 8 opaque string labels (NovaFlow's
action sends `run_id` and `node_id`), echoed in the session status and in
`GET /v1/sessions` (NATS `sessions.list`). A UI that connects after a session
opened lists the sessions and rebinds by its own labels instead of depending on
a one-shot announce.

Event kinds: `status` (first websocket frame only), `run`, `pause_requested`,
`paused`, `step`, `seek`, `speed`, `held_on_io`, `held_on_backstop`, `resolved`,
`arrived`, `fault`, `deadman_expired`, `stopped`, `closed` — one per transition — and
`progress`, the live signal while the robot moves: `location` and
`command_index` (the command being executed, floor of the location), at most
every `ROUTINE_PROGRESS_INTERVAL` (100 ms) and only when the location changed.
A UI moves its scrubber and marks the current row from `progress`; the other
kinds tell it why the run stopped. `stopped` (state `STOPPED`) is the deliberate
stop — its detail says whether standstill was confirmed before the socket
closed; `deadman_expired` (state `FAULTED`) is the stop nobody asked for. Progress events are not written to the
session's log trail.

**Full NATS control**: every operation above is also available as NATS
request/reply on `routine-resolver.v1.…` (see `api/asyncapi.yaml`) — a client
can run the entire control loop (commands + events) without HTTP.

